---
title: Backends and precision
summary: Choosing a CPU or GPU backend, and selecting f64 precision with a process-wide, lock-once float policy.
order: 6
---

## Backends

Every skoro type is generic over a backend `B: skoro::backend::Backend`. The trait is sealed, and two
families implement it:

- `Flex`: burn's CPU backend.
- `CubeBackend<R, F, I>`: burn's cubecl backend on a GPU runtime (e.g. wgpu).

> [!DECISION]
> The `Backend` trait is sealed. See [A sealed backend](../../design/sealed-backend/).

## Precision is a process-wide policy

Float tensors take their precision from the device's *float policy*, and burn locks that policy the
first time any tensor is created on the device. So `f64` has to be chosen **before anything else
runs**:

```
{../../../../crates/skoro-backend/src/policy.rs:11..30}
```

A test binary opts in once, at the top level, with `skoro::backend::f64_test_binary!(Flex)`.
**One binary holds one precision.** A test that needs results at another precision
should compare against constants computed elsewhere, not create tensors at a second precision.

> [!WARNING] `Tensor::from_floats` rounds through f32
> Even on an f64 backend, `Tensor::from_floats` goes through `f32`. Build tensors from `TensorData`
> when precision matters.

> [!DECISION]
> See [Precision is a lock-once policy](../../design/precision-policy/).

## The host boundary

Dense factorizations (Cholesky, triangular solves, QR least squares) run on the host in `f64` via
`skoro-dense`, whatever the backend. Under an `f32` policy that buys `f64` *inside* the solve, but
not end to end. See [Host linear algebra](../../concepts/host-linear-algebra/).

